{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789897517,"nonce":"a07jAioVrx2Bn4eJmuiHqilYOgM=","title":"Linux arm64: the tier thread panics in cranelift-jit's relocation when a host mapping sits between the code and its target","message":"In caribou's process on aarch64 Ubuntu 24.04 (an arm64 container, and the ubuntu-24.04-arm runner), the interop bench's Wren side runs but the tier never installs:\n\n    thread 'beadie-broker' panicked at .../cranelift/jit/src/compiled_blob.rs:222:88:\n    called `Result::unwrap()` on an `Err` value: TryFromIntError(PosOverflow)\n\nThat is `Reloc::Aarch64AdrPrelPgHi21`: `i32::try_from(get_page(what) - get_page(at))`, the `adrp` page offset, which cranelift-jit assumes stays within ±2 GB. Caribou reserves its heap as one mapping of about 2.1 GB (0xffff04963000..0xffff8c7c0000 in the run above), and the JIT's code and the symbol it addresses end up on opposite sides of it. x86_64 and macOS do not see it: there the reservation lands elsewhere in the address space relative to the code.\n\nThe panic is on the tier thread, so the program keeps running interpreted; the bench's Wren→Wren static call reads 139 ns against 5.7 ns for a method call, which is the tier missing. wren_lift alone on the same machine is fine, since nothing large sits between its code and its targets.\n\nReproduce: build caribou (rayzor-blade/caribou) on an arm64 Linux box and run `cargo bench -p caribou-interop --bench interop`.","files":null}]}